/*! \page developer_backend_build_page Explicit Template Instantiation

\ref developer_backend_own_page      (Prev/Up) &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;
\ref developer_backend_device_page  (Next)    &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;
<BR>

The PSCF build system is designed to explicitly instantiation 
specializations of templates for which there are only a few possible 
valid specializations. Specifically, it is designed to explicitly 
instantiate all relevant specializations for class templates that the 
dimension of space and/or a template identifier class as their only 
template parameters. For a class template that takes both the spatial
dimension D and a backend identifier T as template parameters, on a
system that is configured to compile code for both the C++ and Cuda
backends, this can lead to instantiation of up to 6 specializations, 
corresponding to cases with D=1, 2, or 3 and with T=Pscf::CPT or
T=Pscf::CUT.

\section developer_backend_build_file_sec Source File Organization 

The pattern rules used by the PSCF build system require that compilable 
source files that contain only standard C++ have names with a file 
name suffix *.cpp, which are compiled by a standarad C++ compiler, 
but that source files that contain CUDA code have names that end in
a suffix .cu, which are compiled by the nvcc CUDA compiler. This rule
effects the organization files that contain explicit instantiation
declarations for template specializations that take a backend identifier 
class as an argument: Template specializations that take T=Pscf::CPT as 
a template identifier class argument are declared in files with a
file name suffix .cpp, whereas specializations that take T=Pscf::CUT 
must be placed in files with file name suffix .cu.

A class template that takes a backend identifier class as a template
parameter is usually associated with four different C++/CUDA files 
that have a the same base name but different filename suffixes given
by .h, .tpp, .cpp and .cu. We refer to these files in what follows 
as the header (.h) file, implementation (.tpp) file, C++ source
(.cpp) file, and a CUDA source (.cu) file.  The shared base name of 
these four files is always the same as the name of the class template.
For example, the files associated with the class template 
Pscf::Rp::System are located in directory src/rp/system, and are named
   - %System.h
   - %System.tpp
   - %System.cpp
   - %System.cu

The contents of these four files are:
   - The header (.h) file contains the class template definition
   - The implementation (.tpp) contains definitions of member functions
   - The C++  (.cpp) source file contains explicit instantiation 
     definitions for specializations with T=Pscf::CPT
   - The CUDA (.cu) source file contains explicit instantiation 
     definitions for specializations with T=Pscf::CUT

The template implementation (.tpp) file normally includes the header 
(.h) file, while the .cpp and .cu compilable source files each include 
the implementation file. The .cpp file is compiled by a standard C++
compiler, while the .cu file is compiled by the nvcc CUDA compiler.

\section developer_backend_build_conditional_sec Conditional Compilation 

The PSCF build system can be configured to enable or disable use of
particular computational backends. The enabling and disabling of 
backends is controlled by a system of C/C++ preprocessor variables and 
associated makefile variables:

 - Source files with file name suffix .cu, which are only used with the 
   Cuda backend, are all compiled if and only if only if the makefile 
   variable named PSCF_CUDA and a C/C++ preprocessor macro with the 
   same name are both defined.  Among these are all files that contain 
   explicit instantiation definitions for templates that take 
   T = Pscf::CUT as a template argument, as well as the main program 
   file for the pscf_rpg program. 

 - Source files that are used only with the C++ backend are compiled 
   if and only if the preprocessor macro PSCF_CPP and a C/C++ 
   preprocessor macro of the same name are both defined. Among these 
   are all files that contain explicit instantiation definitions for 
   templates that take T=Pscf::CPT as a template argument, as well as 
   the main program file for the pscf_rpc program. 

When both C++ and Cuda backends are enabled, by defining both the 
PSCF_CUDA and PSCF_CPP macros, 6 specializations are instantiated for 
each class template with template parameters D and T, for specializations
with D=1, 2, 3 and two possible values of T. 

\section developer_backend_build_definition_sec Explciit Instantiation Definitions

For .cpp and .cu source files associated with each class template in 
the Pscf::Rp namespace contain explicit template definitions, and 
little else.  For each class template that takes the spatial dimension 
D and a backend identifier class T as parameters, the C++ (.cpp) source 
file contains explicit instantiation definitions for three specializations 
with D=1, 2, and 3 and T=Pscf::CPT. 
The associated CUDA (.cu) source file instead contains instantiation 
defintions for three corresponding specialziations with T=Pscf::CUT. 

As an example, the relevant parts of the System.cpp file in the 
src/rp/system directory are shown below:
\code

#include "Class.tpp"
#include <pscf/backend/cpp/CPT.h>

namespace Pscf {
   namespace Rp {
      template class System<1,CPT>;
      template class System<2,CPT>;
      template class System<3,CPT>;
   }
}
\endcode
The preprocessor include directives shown above include the 
associated class template implementation file, "System.tpp" and 
the header file src/pscf/backend/cpp/CPT.h for backend identifier 
class Pscf::CPT.  

The System.cu file instead contains explicit instantiation 
definitions use Pscf::CUT as the second template argument for 
each specialization, as shown below:
\code

#include "Class.tpp"
#include <pscf/backend/cuda/CUT.h>

namespace Pscf {
   namespace Rp {
      template class System<1,CUT>;
      template class System<2,CUT>;
      template class System<3,CUT>;
   }
}
\endcode

The snippets of System.cpp and System.cu shown above each
exclude a standard file header that identifies each file 
as part of the PSCF package, but show the rest of the full
contents of each file. 

\section developer_backend_build_declaration_sec Explciit Instantiation Declarations

The header file for each class template that is compiled by explicit 
instantiation must contain explicit instantiation declarations for all 
explicitly instantiated specializations of that template.  This is 
necessary to disable implicit template instantiation (which is the 
default in C++), and to enable linking of explicitly instantiated 
template specializations. The explicit instantiation declaration for 
each template specialization is very similar to the corresponding 
explicit instantiation definition, with the word "extern" added to 
the beginning of the declaration. Thus for example,
\code
  extern template class System<3,CUT>
\endcode
is an explicit instantiation declaration for the class template
specializations System<3,CUT>.

To reduce the amount of boilerplace code required in header files,
explicit instantiation declarations are often generated using a 
C/C++ preprocessor macro named PSCF_TMPL_DECLARE.  This is a function
macro that takes the name of a class as an argument, and that is 
designed to expand into a list of all of required template 
instantiation declarations for that class. This PSCF_TMPL_DECLARE
macro is defined in the file src/pscf/backend/tmplDeclare.h.

Header files for most class templates in the src/pscf/rp directory
contain a line containing the PSCF_TMPL_DECLARE macro below the 
class definition, near the bottom of the file.  As an example, the 
relevant parts of the header file src/rp/system/System.h for the 
Pscf::Rp::System class template are shown below in skeleton form:
\code
namespace Pscf {
namespace Rp {

   using namespace Util;
   
   template <int D, class T>
   class System : public ParamComposite
   {
   
      // body of class definition
   
   };
  
   // Inline function definitions
 
   // Forward instantiation declarations
   PSCF_TMPL_DECLARE(System)
   
} // namespace Rp
} // namespace Pscf
\endcode
In this example, the macro
\code
   PSCF_TMPL_DECLARE(System) 
\end
ia expanded by the preprocessor into a block of code that contains 
explicit instantiaion declarations for all specialization of the 
System class template that will be explicitly instantiated by the 
current configuration of the build system. The code obtained by 
preprocessing PSCF_TMPL_DECLARE(System) is equivalent to that which 
would be generated by preprocessing the longer snippet
\code
   #ifdef PSCF_CPP
   extern template class System<1,CPT>;
   extern template class System<2,CPT>;
   extern template class System<3,CPT>;
   #endif
   #ifdef PSCF_CUDA
   extern template class System<1,CUT>;
   extern template class System<2,CUT>;
   extern template class System<3,CUT>;
   #endif
\endcode
The use of a preprocessor macro simply allows this block of 
declarations to be written more concisely header files. 

Explicit instantiation definitions (as opposed to declarations) are 
usually typed out explicitly in the .cpp and .cu files associated with 
each class template, as shown in the excerpts from the System.cpp and 
System.cu files that are displayed above.

<BR>
